batch_2_sections = {
    # PART VIII
    "Competency framework": "(1) The Council shall develop and publish a national competency framework defining the technical, managerial, and ethical standards expected of each category of registered professionals.\n(2) The competency framework shall be updated periodically to reflect advancements in technology, materials, and international best practices in the built environment.",
    "Risk-based regulation": "(1) The regulation of professional work under this Act shall be risk-based, ensuring that the level of regulatory oversight, peer review, and required professional competency is directly proportional to the risk profile of the project.\n(2) The Council shall categorize built environment projects into low, medium, and high-risk classifications as detailed in the Fourth Schedule.",
    "Risk classification": "(1) High-risk projects include, but are not limited to, high-rise buildings, critical infrastructure, public assembly facilities, and complex civil works.\n(2) Low-risk projects include simple residential structures and minor alterations that do not impact structural integrity or public safety. The Cabinet Secretary may gazette amendments to these classifications.",
    "Reserved professional work": "(1) The Council shall publish a schedule of 'reserved professional work' which may only be undertaken, signed, or sealed by a registered professional of a specific discipline.\n(2) Undertaking reserved work without the requisite registration constitutes a severe offence under Part XIV.",
    "Technician and technologist scope": "(1) Registered technicians may independently undertake non-complex, low-risk procedural work and draftsmanship.\n(2) Registered technologists may independently design and supervise medium-risk projects and assist professionals on high-risk projects. They shall not assume principal statutory liability for high-risk reserved work.",
    "Professional scope": "(1) Registered professionals possess the broadest scope of practice and are authorized to independently undertake, design, and supervise high-risk reserved work.\n(2) A professional must not accept an engagement that exceeds their personal competency or the scope of their specific discipline, despite holding a general professional registration.",
    "Specialist scope": "Specialist scopes of work, such as advanced seismic design or geotechnical engineering, shall be strictly reserved for registered specialists. General professionals must engage specialists when a project's complexity mandates it.",
    "Supervision": "(1) A registered professional acting as a principal consultant must provide active, continuous, and verifiable supervision over all technicians, technologists, and graduates working under their mandate.\n(2) The professional remains wholly liable for the technical output and safety of the supervised work.",
    "Delegation": "A professional may delegate specific tasks to competent subordinate staff, provided the delegation is documented and the delegating professional retains ultimate responsibility and signs off on the final output.",
    "Independent checking": "(1) For all high-risk projects, the Council may mandate an independent third-party check of the structural, fire safety, or geotechnical designs by a separate registered professional or specialist.\n(2) The independent checker shall certify the compliance and safety of the design before county approvals are granted.",
    "Peer review": "The Council may institute mandatory peer review mechanisms for complex or novel built environment projects to ensure public safety and foster inter-disciplinary quality assurance.",
    "Practical completion and handover": "(1) The principal professional shall issue a formal Certificate of Practical Completion and Handover when a project reaches practical completion.\n(2) The ten-year decennial liability period shall definitively commence on the date of issuance of the Certificate of Practical Completion and Handover.",
    "Decennial liability and latent defects": "(1) Any registered professional or firm that designs or supervises the construction of a built environment project shall be strictly liable for any structural or life-safety defect (latent defect) that manifests within ten years of the project's practical completion.\n(2) This decennial liability cannot be waived by contract and binds the professional regardless of changes in project ownership.",
    "Statutory duty of care to subsequent owners": "(1) Any registered professional who prepares regulated designs or supervises construction work owes a non-delegable statutory duty of care to both the original client and all subsequent owners of the building.\n(2) This duty requires the professional to exercise reasonable care to avoid economic loss caused by defects, preventing developers from utilizing shell companies to evade liability.",
    "Statutory compliance declarations": "(1) Prior to the issuance of an occupation certificate by a county government, the principal professionals must issue a formal Statutory Compliance Declaration.\n(2) This declaration shall legally certify that the constructed building strictly complies with the approved regulated designs and the National Building Code. Falsifying this declaration is a criminal offence.",
    "Multidisciplinary projects": "(1) On projects requiring multiple built environment disciplines, a 'Lead Professional' shall be designated to coordinate the design and supervise the integration of the various disciplines.\n(2) The designation of a Lead Professional does not absolve the individual discipline professionals (e.g., the structural engineer or MEP engineer) from their specific statutory liabilities.",
    "Digital and BIM practice": "(1) The Council shall establish standards for the use of Building Information Modelling (BIM) and digital practice in the built environment.\n(2) Professionals utilizing BIM must ensure data integrity, clarity of authorship, and secure digital handovers of models to clients and regulators.",

    # PART IX
    "Code of professional conduct": "(1) The Council shall publish a comprehensive Code of Professional Conduct binding on all registered persons and firms.\n(2) The Code shall prescribe the ethical duties owed to the public, clients, employers, and fellow professionals. Breach of the Code constitutes gross professional misconduct.",
    "Public interest": "(1) The paramount duty of every registered professional is to protect the public interest, specifically regarding life, health, public safety, and environmental sustainability.\n(2) This duty to the public overrides any contractual obligation to a client or employer.",
    "Competence": "A professional must only undertake assignments for which they are adequately qualified, experienced, and resourced to execute safely and competently.",
    "Professional independence": "A professional must exercise objective, independent judgment in their practice and must not allow commercial pressures, client demands, or personal biases to compromise safety or technical standards.",
    "Conflicts of interest": "(1) A professional must promptly disclose to their client any actual, potential, or perceived conflict of interest related to an assignment.\n(2) A professional shall not accept remuneration from third parties, such as contractors or suppliers, in connection with services rendered to a client without the client's express written consent.",
    "Confidentiality": "A professional shall maintain the confidentiality of information acquired in the course of their duties and shall not disclose such information without the client's consent, except where disclosure is required by law or to avert a serious risk to public safety.",
    "Client duties": "A professional owes a duty of care, diligence, and good faith to their client, ensuring that services are delivered efficiently and within the agreed scope and budget.",
    "Advertising": "(1) A professional or firm may advertise their services provided the advertising is truthful, dignified, and does not bring the profession into disrepute.\n(2) Advertising must not contain misleading claims, guarantee outcomes, or unfairly criticize other professionals.",
    "Misrepresentation": "A registered person shall not forge, alter, or misrepresent their qualifications, registration category, or the scope of their previous experience in any proposal or curriculum vitae.",
    "Environmental responsibility": "Professionals must advocate for and implement design and construction practices that minimize environmental degradation, promote resource efficiency, and adhere to national environmental laws.",
    "Accessibility": "Professionals must ensure that all designs for public buildings and infrastructure strictly adhere to universal design principles, guaranteeing unhindered accessibility for persons with disabilities.",
    "Professional records": "Professionals must maintain accurate and unadulterated records of all field tests, material approvals, site instructions, and design calculations. Falsifying such records is a severe disciplinary offence.",
    "Duty concerning serious public safety risks": "(1) If a professional identifies a defect, practice, or omission on a project that poses an imminent and serious risk to public safety, they have a statutory duty to immediately notify the project owner, the relevant county authority, and the Council.\n(2) A professional shall not be liable for breach of confidentiality or defamation for making a good-faith report under this section.",
    "Whistleblower protection": "(1) A registered professional who, in good faith, reports a serious breach of safety standards, building codes, or ethical conduct by their employer or client to the Council shall be granted full statutory whistleblower protection.\n(2) It shall be an offence for an employer to dismiss, demote, or otherwise retaliate against a professional for making such a disclosure.",

    # PART X
    "Written terms of engagement": "(1) A professional shall not commence the provision of services to a client without a written, signed agreement defining the terms of engagement.\n(2) The agreement must clearly outline the scope of services, deliverables, timelines, and the basis of fee calculation.",
    "Minimum client information": "Prior to engagement, a professional must provide the client with their registration number, proof of valid practising certificate, and the details of their professional indemnity insurance.",
    "Scope of services": "The Council shall publish standard scopes of services for various built environment professions to serve as a baseline for consumer expectations and to prevent ambiguities in contracts.",
    "Statutory scales of fees": "(1) The Council shall, by notice in the Gazette, prescribe mandatory minimum statutory scales of fees for reserved professional work.\n(2) It shall be professional misconduct to charge fees below the prescribed statutory minimum.",
    "Public procurement guidelines": "(1) All national and county government entities must adhere to the statutory scales of fees and the Council's guidelines when procuring built environment professional services.\n(2) Quality and technical competence, rather than the lowest cost, shall be the primary criteria in evaluating professional service tenders.",
    "Fees and payment terms": "Fees must be charged fairly and transparently. A professional shall not withhold critical safety documents or completion certificates solely to extort disputed fee payments, provided the client has paid for the undisputed completed stages.",
    "Client documents": "(1) A professional must release all final drawings, reports, and statutory approvals to the client upon the settlement of agreed fees.\n(2) The professional retains copyright and intellectual property over their designs, but the client retains the right to use the documents for the specific project they were commissioned for.",
    "Complaints": "The Council shall establish a dedicated consumer protection desk to receive, evaluate, and mediate complaints from the public regarding the services rendered by built environment professionals.",
    "Alternative dispute resolution": "(1) Contracts for professional services must include clauses promoting Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR), such as mediation or arbitration.\n(2) No party shall institute proceedings in any court concerning a professional fee dispute or project defect under this Act unless they have first exhausted the statutory mediation and adjudication mechanisms provided by the Council.",
    "Statutory adjudication": "(1) To protect professionals and contractors from exploitative non-payment, any party to a built environment contract has a statutory right to refer a payment dispute to rapid adjudication.\n(2) The adjudicator must deliver a binding decision within twenty-eight days of appointment, ensuring cash flow is maintained pending any final arbitration or litigation.",
    "Professional insurance information": "A client has a statutory right to request and receive a certified copy of the professional's indemnity insurance policy prior to the commencement of any high-risk project.",
    "Consumer awareness": "The Council shall run continuous public awareness campaigns educating citizens on the importance of engaging registered professionals and the dangers of utilizing quacks.",

    # PART XI
    "Right to complain": "Any person, including a client, a county government official, a fellow professional, or the Council acting on its own motion, may lodge a written complaint against a registered professional or firm alleging professional misconduct.",
    "Receipt and assessment of complaints": "(1) The Registrar shall receive all complaints and conduct an initial assessment to determine if the complaint falls within the jurisdiction of the Council and presents a prima facie case of misconduct.\n(2) Frivolous or vexatious complaints may be dismissed at this stage.",
    "Investigation": "(1) Where a prima facie case exists, the Council shall appoint an Investigation Committee or an independent investigator to gather evidence.\n(2) The investigator shall have the power to interview witnesses, inspect sites, and demand the production of documents.",
    "Investigatory powers": "During an investigation, the Council may compel the production of bank statements, emails, site logs, and design calculations. Refusal to comply with a lawful demand for documents constitutes an offence.",
    "Notice of allegations": "(1) The professional under investigation must be served with a formal Notice of Allegations, detailing the specific charges of misconduct and the evidence against them.\n(2) The professional shall be granted a minimum of twenty-one days to submit a written defense.",
    "Disciplinary Committee": "(1) The Council shall establish an independent Disciplinary Committee composed of senior professionals and at least one Advocate of the High Court of Kenya.\n(2) The Committee shall adjudicate cases of professional misconduct forwarded by the Investigation Committee.",
    "Fair hearing": "(1) The Disciplinary Committee must ensure the accused professional receives a fair hearing, in strict compliance with Article 47 of the Constitution and the Fair Administrative Action Act.\n(2) The accused has the right to be represented by legal counsel and to cross-examine witnesses.",
    "Evidence": "The Disciplinary Committee shall not be strictly bound by the rules of evidence applicable in civil or criminal courts, but must base its decisions on reliable, corroborated, and relevant evidence.",
    "Decisions": "Decisions of the Disciplinary Committee shall be made by majority vote and must be delivered in writing, accompanied by clear, reasoned justifications for the findings.",
    "Sanctions": "Where the Disciplinary Committee finds a professional guilty of misconduct, it may impose one or more of the following sanctions:\n(a) a formal reprimand;\n(b) a financial penalty not exceeding two million shillings;\n(c) mandatory retraining or supervision;\n(d) suspension of the practising certificate for a specified period;\n(e) cancellation of registration and striking off the Register.",
    "Interim measures": "Where an investigation reveals an ongoing and severe threat to public safety, the Council may impose an interim suspension on the professional's practising certificate pending the conclusion of the disciplinary process.",
    "Restoration and reinstatement": "A professional whose registration was cancelled may apply for restoration after a minimum period of three years. The Council must be satisfied that the applicant has rehabilitated and is fit to practice.",

    # PART XII
    "Establishment of the Tribunal": "There is hereby established an independent tribunal to be known as the Built Environment Appeals Tribunal, which shall hear and determine appeals arising from the regulatory decisions of the Council.",
    "Jurisdiction": "The Tribunal shall have jurisdiction to hear appeals concerning:\n(a) the refusal to register an applicant;\n(b) the suspension or cancellation of a registration;\n(c) sanctions imposed by the Disciplinary Committee;\n(d) disputes regarding the accreditation of educational programs.",
    "Composition and appointment": "The Tribunal shall consist of a Chairperson appointed by the Judicial Service Commission and four other members appointed by the Cabinet Secretary from nominees provided by professional associations and consumer protection bodies.",
    "Qualifications of members": "The Chairperson must be an Advocate of the High Court of Kenya with at least ten years' standing. The other members must possess extensive experience in the built environment, public administration, or consumer protection.",
    "Conduct of hearings": "The Tribunal shall conduct its hearings expeditiously and informally, focusing on the substantial merits of the case rather than technicalities.",
    "Powers of the Tribunal": "The Tribunal may confirm, vary, or overturn the decision of the Council, or remit the matter back to the Council for reconsideration with specific directions.",
    "Decisions and awards": "The Tribunal may award costs to the successful party. The decisions of the Tribunal shall be binding and enforceable as if they were decrees of a competent court.",
    "Appeals to the High Court": "Any party aggrieved by a decision of the Tribunal may appeal to the High Court within thirty days, but solely on a point of law."
}
